actual.value:
- Created profiles of all NGOs affected by objections of Turkey and Azerbaijan for delegations in order to build support for their inclusion as observers at CoSP10;
-Close coordination with US, EU delegations on objections, meeting with Group of Friends of Multi-Stakeholder Engagement at UN Vienna;
-Joint press release with Transparency International before the opening of the CoSP10

7 CoSP resolutions drafted, by the UNCAC Coalition, in close collaboration with key partners, resulting in 4 of the drafts being reflected in resolutions that were introduced and adopted: 

Public procurement (led by Open Contracting Partnership);

Beneficial Ownership Transparency (with Open Ownership, TI, TJN);

Whistleblower Protection (led by GAP, Pistaljka);

Gender, inclusion (led by U4);

Written input of the Coalition was also reflected in the Atlanta Declaration.

Drafted, but not introduced by a State: 
Environmental Crime and Corruption (with Wildlife Justice Commission);

Political Finance Transparency (with IFES, TI);

Asset Recovery (with WG on Asset Recovery Chairs).

9+ outputs
An in-person briefing by Monica Kirya (U4, WG on Gender chair) to Vienna delegations in Sept 2023 helped secure support for the first-ever dedicated CoSP10 resolution on gender and corruption
Kirya as WG chair also led drafting of a CoSP10 gender resolution, much of this input was reflected in the zero draft introduced by Ghana
 Advocacy by the WG on Victims helped put the issue in the spotlight at COSP10, with four dedicated events and inclusion in a panel by StAR
Joint CoSP10 written submission by 3 WGs: Consequences of Corruption: The Right of Victims to Participation and Reparation in Corruption Cases; 
Victims: continued efforts to expand and promote the database on national frameworks and case law on legal standing for victims of corruption, now through a dedicated task force; 
Environmental Crime & Corruption WG: open letter supporting a resolution on this topic, signed by 301 CSOs and individuals, did not result in a resolution but contributed to securing a stronger focus on the topic through side events and a high-level event; 
CoSP10 written submission on combatting environmental crime and corruption and communications toolkit;  
Victims of Corruption CoSP10 advocacy toolkit; 
Toolkit: Promoting a gender-sensitive and inclusive approach to anti-corruption (with U4); 
Several WGs have formed task forces, led by different individuals and organizations, to advance data collection, case work, advocacy etc.
An NGO from Yemen highlighted that the network of the WG on Asset Recovery was important for its advocacy against the lifting of sanctions on individuals from Yemen.

Achieved: 45

The Coalition facilitated the participation of 25 civil society representatives from 7 ODA countries (Ukraine, Armenia, Georgia, Nigeria, Mexico, Iraq, Zambia) by including them in our CoSP10 delegation; 
Supported the participation of CSO representatives from Serbia, Indonesia, Nigeria, and Sri Lanka financially; 
Facilitated the participation of 13 CSO representatives from ODA countries (Madagascar, Togo, Namibia, Ghana, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Jordan, Ecuador, Paraguay, Argentina, Mexico, Serbia, Bosnia) by suggesting their participation to UNODC, which covered their travel expenses.

Achieved: 9
Side event on IRM at CoSP10 ;

Statement on IRM delivered in CoSP10 plenary; 

3 written submissions to CoSP10 on IRM; 

Collaboration with UK on IRM initiative, supported by 61 States;

Meeting with Group of Friends of Multistakeholder Engagement in June 2023 on IRM;

Regular meetings with US as incoming CoSP president, including on IRM;

Intervention at high-level roundtable at OGP global summit

Achieved: 
- More than 100 NGO participants, including many from ODA countries, participated in in-person prep workshop on site before the CoSP; 
-3 written submissions: CSOs from Mexico, Yemen, Ghana, made their own CoSP10 written submissions (guidance by the Coalition) 
- CSO from Serbia shaping CoSP10 resolution on whistleblower and co-chairing negotiations with support from the Coalition (in particular its Vice-Chair); 
- Encouraged and helped facilitate CoSP10 plenary statements by CSOs from Yemen (2), Mexico (2), Armenia, Nepal, Madagascar, North Macedonia, Nigeria (3), Azerbaijan, Thailand;
 - featured CSO speakers from Brazil and Sri Lanka in a CoSP10 side event on civic space with the US ambassador to UN Vienna; 
-Co-drafted UNCAC CoSP10 civil society declaration, with input from CSOs from numerous ODA countries, endorsed by 130 CSOs including from South Sudan, DRC, Chad, Namibia, Cameroon, Nepal, Kenya, Ecuador, Panama, Philippines, Ghana, Seychelles, Argentina, Fiji, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Burundi etc. 
- Supported CSOs from Mexico, Nigeria, Kyrgyzstan to organize CoPS10 side events.

7 active working groups being supported by the Coalition.  
1. WG on Victims: 166 members;
2. WG on Environmental Crime & Corr.: 220;
3. WG on Asset Recovery: 134;
4. WG on Gender, Inclusion & Corruption: 180;
5. WG on Grand Corruption: 167;
6. WG on Human Rights & Corr.: 198.
7. WG on Whistleblower Protection: 132

Achieved: 
6 active working groups being supported by the Coalition.  (New WG on Human Rights & Corruption launched around CoSP10.):
1. WG on Victims: 189 members;
2. WG on Environmental Crime & Corr.: 210;
3. WG on Asset Recovery: 155;
4. WG on Gender, Inclusion & Corruption: 176;
5. WG on Grand Corruption: 178;
6. WG on Human Rights & Corr.: 191.

Achieved: Contributed to US CoSP10 Atlanta Declaration which addresses and recognizes the role of civil society in anti-corruption and includes follow-up, e.g. on UNCAC Article 13 implementation.
CoSP10 resolution on procurement, which builds on our input, highlights the monitoring role of civil society;
Worked closely with UK to launch an initiative for a more inclusive UNCAC IRM at CoSP10 that joined by 60 States.
